\DOC induction_shape

\TYPE {induction_shape : thm -> (int * int) list}

\SYNOPSIS
How the goals of an induction come: variables and hypotheses per case.

\DESCRIBE
A call {induction_shape ith} returns one pair per premise of the
induction theorem {ith}: how many variables that premise binds, and how
many hypotheses it has under them. For lists that is {[(0,0); (2,1)]} --
the base case binds nothing and assumes nothing, and the step binds the
head and the tail and assumes the hypothesis about the tail.

Counting the cases off the theorem is what lets a tactic split them
exactly. {REPEAT CONJ_TAC} would go on into a goal that is itself a
conjunction, which is what proving two things by one induction looks
like.

\FAILURE
Fails if {ith} is not of the shape of an induction theorem.

\EXAMPLE
{
  # induction_shape (induction_of `:(A)list`);;
  val it : (int * int) list = [(0, 0); (2, 1)]
}

\USES
{INDUCT_ON_TAC} and {INDUCT_FAMILY_TAC} split their goals with it.

\SEEALSO
INDUCT_FAMILY_TAC, induction_of, INDUCT_ON_TAC.

\ENDDOC
